(Urban Heat Islands: Causes, Health Impacts and Mitigation) Tj
T* (Policy brief prepared for the City Sustainability Committee) Tj
T* (Summary) Tj
T* (Cities are often 1 to 3 degrees C warmer than surrounding rural areas during the day and) Tj
T* (up to 10 degrees C warmer at night. This brief explains why and recommends actions.) Tj
T* (Causes) Tj
T* (- Dark asphalt and roofs absorb sunlight and release heat after sunset.) Tj
T* (- Few trees and little vegetation means less cooling from evapotranspiration.) Tj
T* (- Tall buildings trap heat and block wind in narrow street canyons.) Tj
T* (- Waste heat from air conditioners, vehicles and industry.) Tj
T* (Health impacts) Tj
T* (Heat is the deadliest weather hazard in many countries. Elderly residents, outdoor workers) Tj
T* (and people without air conditioning are most at risk. Neighborhoods with the least tree) Tj
T* (canopy are often lower-income and measurably hotter.) Tj
T* (Mitigation options) Tj
T* (1. Cool roofs: reflective coatings lower roof surface temperature by 20 to 30 degrees C.) Tj
T* (2. Street trees: target 30 percent canopy cover, prioritizing the hottest districts.) Tj
T* (3. Cool pavements and permeable surfaces in parking lots.) Tj
T* (4. Green roofs on public buildings.) Tj
T* (5. Cooling centers and heat warning systems for vulnerable residents.) Tj
T* (Recommendation) Tj
T* (Fund a tree-planting program in the five hottest census tracts and require reflective) Tj
T* (roofing on all new commercial buildings starting next year.) Tj
ET
